[{"learnocaml_version":"2","kind":"exercise","title":"Safe Driving","short_description":"A simple pattern-matching exercise","stars":1,"identifier":"traffic","authors":[["Max Kopinsky","max.kopinsky@mcgill.ca"],["Jacob Errington","jacob.errington@mcgill.ca"]]},{"id":"traffic","prelude":"type traffic_light = Red | Yellow | Green\n","template":"let must_stop (distance : int) (light : traffic_light) : bool =\n  faiwith \"TODO\"\n","descr":[["","<h1 id=\"traffic-safety\">Traffic Safety</h1>\n<p>This problem is a simple exercise with an enumeration type and patter\nmatching.</p>\n<p>You are given the definition\n<code>type traffic_light = Red | Yellow | Green</code>. You can see this\ndefinition in a new pane above the editor to the left. This pane is\ncalled the <em>prelude</em>. You can use anything defined there, but you\ncan't edit it. You <strong>must not</strong> copy the prelude into your\nown code - this would make your code <em>shadow</em> the prelude, and\nthen your code will no longer be compatible with the grader.</p>\n<p>Suppose you are driving and approaching a traffic light. If the light\nis red, you must stop. You must also stop if the light is yellow, when\nyou are more than 100 meters away. Implement the function\n<code>must_stop : int -&gt; traffic_light -&gt; bool</code>, which takes\nyour current distance to the intersection and the color of the light as\narguments. You must use <code>match</code> (or <code>function</code>)\nand must not use <code>if</code>.</p>\n"]],"prepare":"zUf8mNAEVM18xa1dI5QCLyVKXkt+C5tS359TPmx=","test":"","solution":"LvCi7c6ZKyk6Bo+grFeql7xSGQk4W/pZ1oBYWlSpfMBidpZkAWDBXn1veOuetvB47HiTNm2efu76HZZ3e7rK/EIjToAjUdhLo0lhw2AAMet/BucPp3cRmy9RFsV19X0WF2epa2ATFtWV3H31GDE4tuXG6ALIWEHxf5hJ72WOgSkng3fV/V5u4FsbQq+lXw7W0/cNC/BVS/oD","max-score":20,"depend":null,"dependencies":[]},null]